{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 32, :comments ["<b>And you shall take no ransom.</b> And if he fled to the cities of refuge, it is permitted to take him from there and kill him, for even if he seized the horns of the altar, the Merciful One said: ״מעם מזבחי תקחנו למות״. And so we find with Joab, as it is said: ״וינס יואב אל אהל ה' ויחזק בקרנות המזבח״. You find that Joab was a great sage and head of the Sanhedrin, as it is said: ״יושב בשבת תחכמוני ראש השלישי״ — was it that he did not know ״מעם מזבחי תקחנו למות״? Rather, Joab said: Those executed by the court are not buried in their fathers' tombs; it is better that I die and be buried in my fathers' tomb. Immediately: ״וישב בניהו את המלך שלמה לאמר כה דבר יואב וכה ענני״, and he said: ״עשה כאשר דבר ופגע בו וקברתו״, ״והסירותי את דמי עמשא חנם אשר שפך מעלי ומעל בית אבי״. And why was he killed? For so David commanded, as it is written: ״ואתה ידעת את אשר עשה לי יואב בן צרויה״. And what did he do to him? You find that at the time David wrote to Joab: ״תנו את אוריה החתי אל פני המלחמה״ etc., and he did so, and Uriah was killed — all the commanders of the troops gathered against Joab to kill him, for he was chief of the mighty men, as it is written in the book of Samuel: ״ואת אוריה החתי כל אלה שלשים וששה״, and he showed them the letter that David had sent. And this is ״אשר עשה לי יואב בן צרויה״ — that he revealed his secret. And immediately afterward it is written in Samuel: ״ויחר אף ה' בעם ויסת את דוד למנות בישראל״, and Rashi there: I do not know why His anger burned. And one may say that His anger burned on account of Uriah who was killed. And this resolves [the discrepancy] that in Samuel thirty-six mighty men of David are counted, while in Chronicles more are counted — for in the book of Samuel, when he reached Uriah, he interrupted the matter to say that anger came upon him on account of this, whereas in Chronicles he completed the count. A parable: to a king who said to his servants, write for me in a letter all my mighty men and all the mighty deeds they did and will do. They did so and brought the letter before him and read it in his hearing, and each and every one was mentioned by name, and he would ask: Where is he and what is he doing? — until they reached one who was killed through negligence, for lack of aid. The king asked: And where is So-and-so the mighty man? They said to him: He was killed in such and such a manner. Immediately the king grew angry and took the letter from their hand and tore it, and said to them: Because he died through your negligence, three things I impose upon you: either I will starve you, or you shall go to war in a foreign land, or I will strike you a great blow. They replied: We all fall, please, into the hand of my lord the king, for His mercies are many. After they were punished, he commanded to write in a single letter all the mighty men and all their mighty deeds. Thus, when he began to count the mighty men of David and reached Uriah the Hittite, who was killed through David's negligence, he interrupted and wrote ״ויחר אף ה'״, and the Holy One, blessed be He, said to David: Three things I impose upon you: either famine for three years, or war, or pestilence. And in Chronicles it is written: ״או רעב שבע שנים בארצך״. And David said: ״נפלה נא ביד ה' כי רבים רחמיו״, and he chose pestilence, for it is equal for the rich and the poor, the weak and the mighty. For had he chosen famine, Israel would have said: He trusts in his wealth. And had he chosen war, they would have said: He trusts in his mighty men. And after they were punished, in Chronicles the full count of the mighty men was written. And at the end of the earlier verse it is written: ״אשר עשה לי יואב בן צרויה אשר עשה לשני שרי צבאות ישראל״ — to Abner son of Ner and to Amasa son of Jether, and he killed them. And what does this matter have to do with that? For they saw that David commanded Joab to kill Uriah, so they supposed that these too David had commanded to kill, because Abner was Saul's cousin. Therefore David arose and cursed Joab and said: ״אל יכרת מבית יואב זב ומצורע ומחזיק בפלך ונופל בחרבו וחסר לחם״. And then Israel knew that it was not from David to kill Abner. And David commanded Solomon his son to kill Joab, but he himself did not wish to kill him, because he was his sister's son, as it is said: ״ואחיותיהם צרויה ואביגיל״. And at the time Benaiah came to kill him, he said to him: Go and say to Solomon: Do not judge me with two judgments — if you kill me, accept upon yourself the curses that David your father cursed me; or leave me with his curses. Immediately: ״ויאמר לו המלך עשה כאשר דבר ופגע בו וקברתו״. And Rav Yehudah said: All the curses that David cursed Joab were fulfilled in the seed of David: זב — from Rehoboam, as it is written: ״והמלך רחבעם התאמץ לעלות במרכבה״, and it is written: ״כל המרכב אשר ירכב עליו הזב״. מצורע — from Uzziah, as it is written: ״ויהי המלך עוזיהו מצורע עד יום מותו״. מחזיק בפלך — from Asa, as it is written: ״לעת זקנתו חלה את רגליו״ — this teaches that he was afflicted with pedagra. נופל בחרב — from Josiah, as it is written: ״ויורו המורים למלך יאשיהו״, and in tractate Ta'anit it is concluded that they pierced him with three hundred iron spears until his entire body became like a sieve. חסר לחם — from Jehoiachin, as it is said: ״וארוחתו ארוחת תמיד נתנה לו מאת המלך כל ימי חייו״ — from the table of Evil-Merodach."], :book :numbers, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 35, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:10:52.984884450Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}